var e=`# CHAPTER 1 — Joaquim

You probably know me.

I'm Kim. My name is Joaquim, but nobody's called me that in a long time. It's Kim, the nickname, and that's fine.

Today I'm going to tell you something that might explain why Jack insists so hard that we have a band ([he was already into it, main line, ch. 1](/historias/lutas-de-ilusao/capitulo-01)).

Fair warning: it's a pretty dumb story. But okay.

---

I've known Jack since I was seven.

We ran into each other on the street one day ([Alan has his own version of this street life — the Champion's story, ch. 8](/historias/contos/02/08)). Literally ran into each other — both of us turning the same corner from opposite directions, both looking over our shoulders. He'd just come out of a fight. I'd just come out of another one. Both of us with a short fuse, faces still hot.

One looked sideways at the other. That was enough.

It ended on the ground, the two of us going at each other over nothing — no reason, no crowd, no prize. And since that day we never stopped fighting. It's the most constant thing in my life after the bus schedule.

Maybe you don't know this, but Jack has a ritual.

Around when we were nine there was a guy we always faced — and we faced him together ([Kim still talks about him today, main line, ch. 3](/historias/lutas-de-ilusao/capitulo-03) — and the Champion's story tells this ritual from the inside, [ch. 14](/historias/contos/02/14)). Every month, same day, same place. We'd go up, take a beating, come down. And the next month we'd go up again.

That's where Jack and I learned the most useful thing we know how to do: survive a fight covering each other. It wasn't training. There was no teacher. It was just taking beatings together enough times until our two bodies started moving as if they were run by a single mind.

From that first fight on the corner, at seven, nobody came out on top. There were some others and yeah, I have to admit, Jack won a few, I won a few, he's probably ahead — by his count, anyway.

But it wasn't just brutality that came out of that ritual.

---

Some people like to say that what Jack and I have is friendship.

It's bigger than that.

You don't choose a blood brother. It's a lottery. You're born and there it is. Not Jack. Jack I chose. Very small, without really knowing what I was choosing, but I chose him.

We never said that to each other out loud. We don't talk about that kind of thing. If one of us started talking about feelings, the other would ask if he'd hit his head. But we both know.

Back then it was just me and Jack against the world. Then Nina showed up, and other things happened, and the group changed. But at that time, other than Helena, the only person I had real contact with in my life was Jack.

"Against the world" isn't a figure of speech. We split food when one of us didn't have any. Slept on the school roof when we couldn't go home. One knew what day the other was going to get beaten and showed up without arranging it. It wasn't pretty. But it was solid.

A brotherhood we chose to have.

---

I'm not one to make friends. Never was.

It's not sensitivity, it's not trauma, it's not any of that stuff the movies like. It's math. Human beings are complicated, a person can let you down, can disappear, can sell you out. The fewer people with that kind of access to you, the less chance of a knife in the back. I learned that before I learned to add.

With Jack the math came out different.

I spent years waiting for him to let me down, disappear, sell me out. Testing him without him knowing it was a test. And he never failed. Not once. I got tired of waiting.

So fine. He's a brother. Chosen, tested, approved. End of discussion.

And it was that brotherhood that got me a band without asking me.
`;export{e as default};
